## MCP (Model Context Protocol) Integration

Serial Studio includes a built-in **MCP (Model Context Protocol) server** that exposes the API Server functionality to AI models like Claude and ChatGPT. AI assistants can control Serial Studio directly (connecting to devices, reading sensor data, sending commands, and managing exports) through natural language instructions.

# Actions

## Overview

Your sketch reads a line from `Serial` and resets itself when it sees `RST`. In a serial monitor you would type `RST` and press Enter. An action puts that same command on a dashboard button.

```cpp
void loop() {
  if (Serial.available()) {
    String cmd = Serial.readStringUntil('\n');
    if (cmd == "RST") resetDevice();
  }
}
```

To make the button, create an action with these fields in the Project Editor:

| Property             | Value           |
|----------------------|-----------------|
| Action Title         | Reset Device    |
| Transmit Data        | `RST`           |
| End-of-Line Sequence | New Line (`\n`) |

```json
"sounds": {
  "sequence": "M",
  "channels": {
    "Engine": { "warning": "sounds/horn.wav", "caution": "sounds/chime.wav" }
  }
}
```

A relative path resolves against the project file's folder, so a project folder can be shared with its sounds. A file that no longer exists falls back to the bundled sound and raises a Problem Center warning naming the path.

## API

These commands need the API Server, which is off by default (port 7777); see [API Reference](API-Reference.md).

A whole script, drawing one bar that follows the first value. `paint` runs on every screen refresh; `ctx` is the drawing surface, and `w` and `h` are the widget size in pixels.

```javascript
function paint(ctx, w, h) {
  ctx.fillStyle = theme.widget_base;
  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);

if (datasets.length === 0) return;

const ds = datasets[0];
  const v  = (ds.value - ds.min) / (ds.max - ds.min || 1);

ctx.fillStyle = theme.widget_highlight;
  ctx.fillRect(8, h - v * (h - 16) - 8, w - 16, v * (h - 16));
}
```

## Adding a Canvas widget to a project

Canvas draws datasets, and datasets come from your project's frame parser. Start from a project that already shows your `Serial.println` values (see [Getting Started](Getting-Started.md) and [Frame Parser Scripting](Frame-Parser.md)). Add one dataset per value, so `Serial.println(level);` needs one and `Serial.print(a); Serial.print(','); Serial.println(b);` needs two. [Project Editor](Project-Editor.md) covers adding datasets.

```javascript
const HISTORY = 256;
const trace   = [];

function onFrame() {
  if (datasets.length === 0) return;
  trace.push(datasets[0].value);
  if (trace.length > HISTORY) trace.shift();
}

function paint(ctx, w, h) {
  const ds = datasets[0];
  const span = (ds.max - ds.min) || 1;

ctx.fillStyle = theme.widget_base;
  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);

if (trace.length < 2) return;

ctx.strokeStyle = theme.widget_highlight;
  ctx.lineWidth   = 2;
  ctx.beginPath();
  for (let i = 0; i < trace.length; ++i) {
    const x = (i / (HISTORY - 1)) * w;
    const y = h - ((trace[i] - ds.min) / span) * h;
    i === 0 ? ctx.moveTo(x, y) : ctx.lineTo(x, y);
  }
  ctx.stroke();
}
```

```javascript
const traces = [];
function onFrame() {
  while (traces.length < datasets.length) traces.push([]);
  for (let i = 0; i < datasets.length; ++i) {
    traces[i].push(datasets[i].value);
  }
}
```

## Globals

Five globals are available inside `paint()` and `onFrame()`: `datasets`, `group`, `frame`, `theme` and `console`. The control functions are listed in [Control APIs](#control-apis--devicewrite-and-actionfire).

### `datasets`

An array-like view of the group's datasets. `datasets.length` returns the count, `datasets[i]` returns the i-th dataset. Each dataset is a frozen object with the fields below. The fields are getters and always return the current value.

```javascript
var alarmRaised = false;
function onFrame() {
  var high = datasets[0].value > 100;
  if (high && !alarmRaised) actionFire(7);   // existing "Alarm" action
  alarmRaised = high;
}
```

The dashboard helpers (`clearPlots()`, `setPlotPoints(n)`, `setTerminalVisible(bool)`, `setNotificationLogVisible(bool)`, `setClockVisible(bool)`, `setStopwatchVisible(bool)`, `setActiveWorkspace(idOrName)`) are also available, with the same `{ ok, error }` return shape and the same "fire once on a state transition" guidance. Do not call them from `paint()`.

See [Frame Parser Scripting](Frame-Parser.md) for full signatures and failure modes shared across parsers, transforms, and canvas scripts.

```javascript
ctx.beginPath();
ctx.moveTo(cx + Math.cos(startA) * r, cy + Math.sin(startA) * r);
ctx.arc(cx, cy, r, startA, endA);
ctx.stroke();
```

For full circles, `moveTo(cx + r, cy)` is sufficient.

Pixel coordinates address the boundaries between pixels. A 1-pixel line at integer Y is split between two rows and rendered as a 2-pixel anti-aliased band. Offset by 0.5 (`ctx.moveTo(x, y + 0.5)`) to get a crisp line.

### Direct shapes

`fillRect(x, y, w, h)`, `strokeRect(x, y, w, h)`, `clearRect(x, y, w, h)`. These bypass the path and are appropriate for backgrounds and grids.

### Text

`fillText(text, x, y)`, `strokeText(text, x, y)`, `measureTextWidth(text)`, `measureText(text)`. Honors the current `font`, `textAlign`, and `textBaseline`. `measureTextWidth` returns the advance width as a plain number; `measureText` returns a metrics object with `width`, `actualBoundingBoxAscent`, `actualBoundingBoxDescent`, `fontBoundingBoxAscent`, and `fontBoundingBoxDescent`. For centered text, prefer `textAlign = "center"` over measuring.

To place a value and a unit label side by side, measure the value with the value's own font:

```javascript
ctx.font = "bold 18px sans-serif";
const valueW = ctx.measureTextWidth(value);
ctx.fillText(value, x, y);

ctx.fillStyle = theme.placeholder_text;
ctx.font      = "10px sans-serif";
ctx.fillText(units, x + valueW + 6, y);
```

### Images

`drawImage(src, x, y)` and `drawImageScaled(src, x, y, w, h)`. The `src` string is resolved through a sandbox: `qrc:/` resources and paths inside the project file's directory are accepted. Other paths are rejected, and a rejected path is silently skipped: nothing is drawn and no exception is raised.

```javascript
ctx.drawImage("logo.png", 12, 12);                                // relative to the project
ctx.drawImage("qrc:/iconpacks/fine-line/widgets/48/painter.svg",  // bundled resource
              0, 0);
```

N means any number of datasets, with the template adapting to the group size. 1+ means at least one. Templates are stored as plain `.js` files under `app/rcc/scripts/painter/`.

## Layout tips

None of this is enforced by the engine. It keeps a custom widget looking like the built-in ones.

A card with a soft shadow and a 1-pixel border holds the content. The 0.5-pixel offset on `strokeRect` aligns the stroke to a single pixel column.

```javascript
ctx.fillStyle = theme.widget_base;
ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);

const pad = 14;
ctx.fillStyle = theme.widget_border;      // shadow
ctx.fillRect(pad + 1, pad + 2, w - pad * 2, h - pad * 2);
ctx.fillStyle = theme.alternate_base;     // card body
ctx.fillRect(pad, pad, w - pad * 2, h - pad * 2);
ctx.strokeStyle = theme.widget_border;
ctx.lineWidth = 1;
ctx.strokeRect(pad + 0.5, pad + 0.5, w - pad * 2 - 1, h - pad * 2 - 1);
```

Size from the available area. Compute it first, fit content into the smaller dimension, and center horizontally. Fixed fractions like `cy = h * 0.55` fit one aspect ratio and break at others.

```javascript
const labelH = 36;                          // reserved for bottom labels
const margin = 12;
const availW = w - margin * 2;
const availH = h - margin * 2 - labelH;
const r      = Math.max(8, Math.min(availW, availH) * 0.5 - 8);
const cx     = w / 2;
const cy     = margin + r + 8;
```

## Examples

### Tank level with alarm coloring

The fill color switches from the highlight color to the accent color near the top of the range, and to the alarm color once the value reaches the dataset's `alarmHigh`.

```javascript
function paint(ctx, w, h) {
  ctx.fillStyle = theme.widget_base;
  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);

if (datasets.length === 0) return;

const ds   = datasets[0];
  const v    = Number.isFinite(ds.value) ? ds.value : ds.min;
  const span = (ds.max - ds.min) || 1;
  const norm = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (v - ds.min) / span));

let color = theme.widget_highlight;
  if (Number.isFinite(ds.alarmHigh) && v >= ds.alarmHigh) color = theme.alarm;
  else if (norm > 0.85) color = theme.accent;

const pad = 16;
  const th  = h - pad * 2 - 24;
  ctx.fillStyle = theme.alternate_base;
  ctx.fillRect(pad, pad, w - pad * 2, th);

ctx.fillStyle = color;
  ctx.fillRect(pad, pad + th * (1 - norm), w - pad * 2, th * norm);

ctx.fillStyle    = theme.widget_text;
  ctx.font         = "bold 16px sans-serif";
  ctx.textAlign    = "center";
  ctx.textBaseline = "alphabetic";
  ctx.fillText(v.toFixed(1) + " " + (ds.units || ""), w / 2, h - 8);
}
```

### Polar bug indicator

Two datasets, bearing in degrees and range in 0..1, plotted as a marker on a polar grid. Each `arc()` is preceded by a `moveTo()` to its start point.

```javascript
function paint(ctx, w, h) {
  ctx.fillStyle = theme.widget_base;
  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);

if (datasets.length < 2) return;

const cx = w / 2;
  const cy = h / 2;
  const r  = Math.min(w, h) * 0.42;

// Concentric range rings.
  ctx.strokeStyle = theme.widget_border;
  ctx.lineWidth   = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i <= 4; ++i) {
    const rr = (r * i) / 4;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(cx + rr, cy);
    ctx.arc(cx, cy, rr, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.stroke();
  }

// Bearing spokes every 30 degrees.
  ctx.strokeStyle = theme.widget_border;
  for (let deg = 0; deg < 360; deg += 30) {
    const a = (deg - 90) * Math.PI / 180;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(cx, cy);
    ctx.lineTo(cx + Math.cos(a) * r, cy + Math.sin(a) * r);
    ctx.stroke();
  }

// Marker.
  const bearing = datasets[0].value;
  const range   = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, datasets[1].value));
  const a       = (bearing - 90) * Math.PI / 180;
  const x       = cx + Math.cos(a) * r * range;
  const y       = cy + Math.sin(a) * r * range;

ctx.fillStyle = theme.widget_highlight;
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.moveTo(x + 6, y);
  ctx.arc(x, y, 6, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fill();

// Readout.
  ctx.fillStyle    = theme.widget_text;
  ctx.font         = "bold 12px sans-serif";
  ctx.textAlign    = "center";
  ctx.textBaseline = "alphabetic";
  ctx.fillText(bearing.toFixed(0) + "°  /  " + (range * 100).toFixed(0) + "%",
               cx, h - 10);
  ctx.textAlign = "start";
}
```

## The decoder script

The decoder script defines one global object named `decoder` with three members. This one marks every line the device sends and the newline that ends it:

```javascript
decoder = {
  rows: ["bytes", "packets"],
  classes: [
    {name: "payload", color: "#59a14f"},
    {name: "newline", color: "#e15759"}
  ],
  decode: function(bytes, offset, ctx, size) {
    const b = new Uint8Array(bytes)
    let i = 0
    while (i < size) {
      const start = i
      while (i < size && b[i] !== 0x0A) ++i
      if (i >= size) return start
      ctx.annotate(offset + start, offset + i - 1, 1, 0,
                   [(i - start) + " bytes", "P"])
      ctx.annotate(offset + i, offset + i, 0, 1, ["LF", "n"])
      ++i
    }

The Control Loop runs only while a project file is loaded and a device is connected. It does not run in Quick Plot or console-only mode, or while a CSV, MDF4 or session file is open for replay.

## The lifecycle functions

```javascript
function onConnect() {
  // Optional. Runs once, just BEFORE the connection opens.
}

function setup() {
  // Runs once, right AFTER the device connects.
}

function loop() {
  // Runs repeatedly while the device stays connected. Pace it with delay().
}
```

Define `setup()`, `loop()`, or both. A device that streams on its own after a one-time handshake needs only `setup()`; a device that must be polled needs `loop()`. `onConnect()` is optional and cannot stand alone: a script with neither `setup()` nor `loop()` fails with "Script must define setup() and/or loop()." The script is restarted on each new connection, so `setup()` runs again every time you reconnect.

function loop() {
  tick += 1;
  // ... use tick ...
}
```

`onConnect()` is for the rare case where something must be running before Serial Studio opens the connection, such as a helper process; see [Launching helper processes](#launching-helper-processes-the-system-api).

### loop() pacing with delay()

Call `delay(ms)` at the end of `loop()`, as in an Arduino sketch. Without it, `loop()` is queued again 1 ms after each call returns, and every I/O, table, or dashboard call in it crosses to the GUI thread, so an unpaced loop that writes on every pass can make the interface sluggish.

`delay(ms)` blocks only the script's own worker thread, never the dashboard or frame parsing. It is clamped to 0 to 3,600,000 ms (one hour), and the 2000 ms limit starts over when it returns.

## Examples

### 1. Periodic keep-alive

A device that disconnects when idle gets a heartbeat byte every second.

```javascript
function loop() {
  io.writeData("00", SerialStudio.Hex);   // single 0x00 byte
  delay(1000);
}
```

### 2. Init commands that need spacing

A device that needs its init commands spaced out can use `delay()` between them, right inside `setup()`.

```javascript
function setup() {
  var cmds = ["BEGIN\n", "MODE 1\n", "START\n"];
  for (var i = 0; i < cmds.length; ++i) {
    io.writeData(cmds[i], SerialStudio.Text);
    delay(250);
  }
}
```

### 3. Writing only while the link is up

`loop()` runs only while the device is connected, but the link can drop or be paused between two ticks. Check `io.getStatus()` before writing and skip the pass when the link is down.

```javascript
function loop() {
  var status = io.getStatus();
  if (!status.ok || !status.result.isConnected)
    return;

io.writeData("POLL", SerialStudio.Text);
  delay(500);
}
```

### 4. BLE wake-up handshake

Many BLE devices expose a service (here `FFF0`) with separate write (`FFF1`) and notify (`FFF2`) characteristics, and only start streaming after a command sequence is written. The service and notify characteristic are chosen on the BLE source and saved with the project; the driver wires them on connect, so the control loop only writes the enable sequence. Readings then arrive through the frame parser like any other data.

```javascript
function setup() {
  var cmds = ["0102030405", "0607080900", "0A0B0C0D0E"];
  for (var i = 0; i < cmds.length; ++i) {
    var r = io.ble.writeCharacteristic("fff1", cmds[i], SerialStudio.Hex);
    if (!r.ok)
      console.log("Handshake step " + i + " failed: " + r.error);
  }
}
```

The characteristic UUID is a plain string and the payload a hex string with `SerialStudio.Hex`; do not wrap the arguments in an object and do not hand-encode base64. For a binary BLE device, set the source's decoder to **Binary (raw bytes)** with **No delimiters (whole chunk)** so the frame parser receives each notification as an array of byte values; see [Frame Parser Reference](Frame-Parser.md) for reading bytes and decoding floats.

### 5. Bringing a serial device up again (advanced)

This one is only for UART devices that need a special start-up sequence. A UART source with a [Connection Sequence](Drivers-UART.md#connection-sequence) runs it on every connect, before `setup()`. A device that resets itself while the port stays open, for example on a supply dip, goes silent until it is brought up again. The loop below watches the frame age and re-runs the sequence on the open port; `io.uart.sendBreak(55)` alone serves a device that needs only a BREAK reset.

```javascript
function loop() {
  var latest = io.getLatestFrame();
  if (latest.ok && latest.result.hasData && latest.result.ageMs > 3000) {
    var r = io.uart.runSequence();
    if (!r.ok)
      console.log("Re-run refused: " + r.error);

delay(5000);
    return;
  }

delay(500);
}
```

`io.uart.runSequence()` returns as soon as the run starts, so it never counts against the 2000 ms limit; the sequence reports its outcome in the console. Give it time with `delay()` before checking again, longer than the sequence's time budget. `io.uart.runSequence()` and `io.uart.sendBreak()` each take an optional `{ sourceId: n }` for a Device other than the first (source 0).

### 6. Self-building dashboard

This example uses `newFrame()` and `ensureDashboard()`, both explained under [Reading data](#reading-data-see-decide-act). A logger that streams a variable number of comma-separated channels gets a plotted dataset per channel, created the moment a new channel first appears. Widgets that already exist are left alone, so the script is safe to leave running.

```javascript
function loop() {
  var f = newFrame();
  if (!f) { delay(100); return; }

var spec = { title: "Telemetry", widget: "multiplot", datasets: [] };
  for (var i = 0; i < f.values.length; ++i)
    spec.datasets.push({ title: "Channel " + (i + 1), index: i + 1, plot: true });

ensureDashboard([spec]);
  delay(100);
}
```

## Reading data: See, Decide, Act

A control loop can also read what the device sends and act on it: **see** the latest data, **decide** in plain JavaScript, **act** through the SDK.

```javascript
function loop() {
  var f = newFrame();
  if (f && f.values[0] > 80)
    io.writeData("FAN ON\n", SerialStudio.Text);

delay(200);
}
```

- **See**: `newFrame()` returns the latest frame received from the device, or `null` when nothing new has arrived since the last call. It also returns `null` before the first frame of a connection, so check for that before reading `values`. `newFrame(sourceId)` takes an optional source, but the SDK remembers one sequence number for all sources, so poll a single source per script, or compare `f.sequence` yourself using `io.getLatestFrame({ sourceId })`.
- **Decide**: plain JavaScript. Compare, count, branch; top-level variables persist across `loop()` calls.
- **Act**: write to the device (`io.writeData`), post notifications, or reshape the dashboard ([`ensureDashboard()`](#ensuredashboardspec-declarative-widgets), or any `project.*` call).

### Staleness watchdog

`ageMs` makes a communication-loss watchdog short: no clock mixing, no timestamp bookkeeping in the parser. Mark the failure state in the Variables tables, then `refreshDashboard()` renders it without waiting for a frame:

```javascript
var commLost = false;

function loop() {
  var r = io.getLatestFrame();
  var stale = r.ok && r.result.hasData && r.result.ageMs > 1000;

if (stale && !commLost) {
    commLost = true;
    tableSet("BRD-1", "boot_selftest", 0xCC);  // project-specific: this project's transforms treat 0xCC as comm loss
    refreshDashboard();
    notifyCritical("Watchdog", "No frames for " + r.result.ageMs + " ms");
  } else if (!stale && commLost) {
    commLost = false;
    notifyInfo("Watchdog", "Communication restored");
  }

delay(100);
}
```

`notifyInfo()`, `notifyWarning()` and `notifyCritical()` need Pro. Without a license they throw an error, so drop the two notify calls to run this example in the free edition.

### ensureDashboard(spec): declarative widgets

`ensureDashboard(spec)` makes the described dashboard exist. You state what you want; it creates only what is missing and never modifies what is already there. Calling it on every `loop()` pass with the same spec is free (the last satisfied spec is remembered), so there is no bookkeeping to write:

```javascript
ensureDashboard([
  {
    title: "Telemetry",            // group, matched by title
    widget: "multiplot",           // datagrid | multiplot | gps | none | ...
    datasets: [
      { title: "Temperature", index: 1, plot: true, units: "C" },
      { title: "Pressure",    index: 2, plot: true, gauge: true }
    ]
  }
]);
```

A typical launcher pairs `system.projectDir()` with `system.exec()` and tries `python3` first, falling back to `python`:

```javascript
function startHelper() {
  var dir = system.projectDir();
  if (!dir.ok)
    return;

var script = dir.result.directory + "/generator.py";
  var r = system.exec("python3", { args: ["-u", script], workingDir: dir.result.directory });
  if (!r.ok)
    r = system.exec("python", { args: ["-u", script], workingDir: dir.result.directory });
}
```

Pass `-u` (as above) when launching Python so its output is unbuffered and appears in the console line by line instead of in bursts.

**JavaScript: declare `var` at the top of the file:**

```javascript
var alpha = 0.1;
var ema;

function transform(value) {
  if (ema === undefined)
    ema = value;

ema = alpha * value + (1 - alpha) * ema;
  return ema;
}
```

Serial Studio wraps each JavaScript transform so top-level `var` declarations belong to that dataset, not to the shared engine.

### Bare globals in JavaScript

An implicit global in JavaScript is written to the shared engine's global object, so another dataset with the same mistake clobbers it:

```javascript
// WRONG: ema has no var, so it is a global on the shared engine.
function transform(value) {
  if (typeof ema === 'undefined') ema = value;
  ema = 0.1 * value + 0.9 * ema;
  return ema;
}
```

**Lua example: scale a voltage reading by a project-wide calibration factor:**

```lua
function transform(value)
  local k = tableGet("calibration", "voltage_scale")  -- constant
  return value * (k or 1.0)
end
```

**JavaScript example: same idea.**

```javascript
function transform(value) {
  var k = tableGet("calibration", "voltage_scale");
  return value * (k !== undefined ? k : 1.0);
}
```

**Writing to a computed variable. One transform publishes, another consumes:**

```lua
-- Dataset 10 (processed first): publish the total current
function transform(value)
  tableSet("runtime", "total_current", value)
  return value
end
```

```lua
function transform(value, info)
  -- info.frameNumber : integer, monotonic counter per source (starts at 1)
  -- info.sourceId    : integer, the source the dataset belongs to
  -- info.timestampMs : integer, monotonic milliseconds (steady clock)
  return value
end
```

```javascript
function transform(value, info) {
  // info.frameNumber : number
  // info.sourceId    : number
  // info.timestampMs : number
  return value;
}
```

`info.timestampMs` is a **monotonic** millisecond counter taken from the OS steady clock. It increases between consecutive frames but is not wall-clock time and does not match `Date.now()`. Use it for deltas (`info.timestampMs - lastTs`), not for "what time is it now".

The transform returns the raw temperature unchanged (so the dashboard still shows the measured value) and writes the computed PWM duty cycle back on every frame.

### JavaScript example: alarm latch

```javascript
var triggered = false;

function transform(value) {
  if (!triggered && value > 100) {
    var r = deviceWrite("ALARM=1\n");
    if (r.ok) triggered = true;
    else console.warn("alarm write failed:", r.error);
  }
  return value;
}
```

The `triggered` variable stops `deviceWrite` from firing again on later frames once the alarm has latched.

### Targeting another source

Pass an explicit `sourceId` to write to a different source. Useful when telemetry arrives on one source and the device's command channel lives on another:

The Control Loop is a free feature: one `setup()` / `loop()` JavaScript script per project, found under **Project Scripts** in the Project Editor tree. `setup()` runs once and `loop()` repeats until you disconnect. It runs beside the frame pipeline and suits work that is not tied to a single frame: the handshake that makes a silent device start sending, polling, watchdogs, and values that depend on time. The example below only writes a Variable and does not touch the device; to send a command to the device, see [Control Loop](Control-Loop.md).

```javascript
function loop() {
  tableSet("sim", "temp", 20 + 5 * Math.sin(Date.now() / 5000));
  refreshDashboard();
  delay(100);
}
```

```javascript
function loop() {
  tableSet("sim", "temp", 20 + 5 * Math.sin(Date.now() / 5000));
  dashboardTick();
  delay(100);
}
```

It re-runs the transforms, publishes the result to the dashboard, and sends the frame to every enabled export sink (CSV, MDF4, Historian, MQTT, InfluxDB, the API stream), so the script's values are recorded like device data. A sink that is off records nothing. `dashboardTick()` is JavaScript only and is available in the Control Loop and in JavaScript macros. It publishes only in a project-file session with at least one group; in Quick Plot or Console mode, or when no dataset value changed since the last tick, it returns `published: false` and records nothing.

# Device Declarations

Your Arduino prints values with `Serial.println`. A device declaration lets it also tell Serial Studio how to draw them: titles, units, ranges and widgets, sent as one line of text. Serial Studio builds the dashboard from that line on connect, with no project file and without opening the Project Editor.

The SerialStudio device library writes the line for you in Arduino C++, plain C and Python. This sketch declares a GPS map:

```cpp
#include <SerialStudio.h>

SerialStudio studio(Serial, "TinyGPS");

void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); }

void loop() {
  studio.poll();

studio.group(F("GPS Map"), F("gps"));
  studio.channel(F("Latitude"), 48.8584, 4).units(F("deg")).widget(F("lat")).graph(false);
  studio.channel(F("Longitude"), 2.2945, 4).units(F("deg")).widget(F("lon")).graph(false);
  studio.channel(F("Altitude"), 35.0, 1).units(F("m")).widget(F("alt")).graph(false);
  studio.send();
}
```

The first pass through `loop()` sends the declaration. Every later pass sends a frame with the current values. Install the library first (see [Arduino](#arduino)).

In Serial Studio:

1. In the **Device Setup** panel, set **Operation Mode** to **Quick Plot (Auto-Detected Format)** (see [Operation Modes](Operation-Modes.md)).
2. Select the serial port.
3. Set the baud rate to 115200 to match `Serial.begin`.
4. Press **Connect**.

The controls appear on the dashboard as [Output Controls](Output-Controls.md). Operating one makes Serial Studio send a line such as:

```
#ss!Speed=128
```

In an Arduino sketch, the control goes in a control group next to at least one channel, and `onControl()` receives the name and value:

```cpp
int onSpeed(const char* name, double value) { analogWrite(9, (int)value); return 0; }

studio.onControl(onSpeed);
studio.controlGroup(F("Controls"));
studio.control(F("Speed"), SS_CONTROL_SLIDER);
```

The callback returns `0` to accept the command. A control title is 1 to 32 characters from `A-Z`, `a-z`, `0-9`, space, `_`, `.` and `-`, with no leading, trailing or doubled spaces; an invalid title refuses the whole declaration. When Serial Studio tracks replies, it appends `@` and a token to the value and the library strips it. The token is appended to text frames only.

The Arduino examples are in `firmware/arduino/examples`, and the [MPU6050](../../examples/MPU6050/README.md) and [TinyGPS](../../examples/TinyGPS/README.md) examples each have a declared sketch.

### C

Copy `firmware/c/serialstudio.c`, `serialstudio.h` and `ss_vocabulary.h` into the project. The file is C99. Output goes through a write callback; input arrives through `ss_feed()`.

```c
/* write_to_uart(const uint8_t* data, size_t len) is your own UART send function. */
ss_t ss;
ss_init(&ss, write_to_uart, NULL, "TinyGPS");

ss_group(&ss, "GPS Map", "gps");
ss_channel_double(&ss, "Latitude", 48.8584, 4);
ss_units(&ss, "deg");
ss_widget(&ss, "lat");
ss_send(&ss);
```

## Quick start

The firmware needs to advertise a device name and notify one characteristic with lines such as `23.4,51.0`. This ESP32 Arduino sketch does that once per second:

```cpp
#include <BLEDevice.h>
#include <BLE2902.h>

BLECharacteristic* tx;

void setup() {
  BLEDevice::init("SensorNode");
  BLEServer* server = BLEDevice::createServer();
  BLEService* svc = server->createService("FFE0");
  tx = svc->createCharacteristic("FFE1", BLECharacteristic::PROPERTY_NOTIFY);
  tx->addDescriptor(new BLE2902());
  svc->start();
  BLEDevice::getAdvertising()->addServiceUUID("FFE0");
  BLEDevice::startAdvertising();
}

void loop() {
  tx->setValue("23.4,51.0\n");
  tx->notify();
  delay(1000);
}
```

`FFE0` is the service and `FFE1` the characteristic. They are arbitrary UUIDs the sketch chooses, and Serial Studio shows them in its dropdowns. The sketch uses the BLE library bundled with the ESP32 Arduino core. It does not restart advertising after a disconnect, so reset the board between sessions. Keep each notification at 20 bytes or fewer, the payload of the default MTU (see [MTU and throughput](#mtu-and-throughput)).

Then, in Serial Studio:

```js
function parse(frame) {
  const id  = ((frame[0] & 0x07) << 8) | frame[1];
  const dlc = frame[2];
  if (id !== 0x100 || dlc < 1)
    return [];
  return [frame[3]];
}
```

The first byte holds ID bits 10 to 8, hence the `0x07` mask. The returned array becomes dataset 1, which you add in the Project Editor.

In a DBC-imported project the auto-generated parser is a Lua frame parser built from the DBC: a declarative per-message spec plus a generic decoder that dispatches by CAN ID, extracts each signal at the documented bit offset, applies factor and offset, and publishes the value into the matching dataset. The imported source starts on the Lua platform. When the project is built by hand, the dispatch logic can be written in Lua or JavaScript; see [Frame Parser Scripting](Frame-Parser.md).

Writing: text sent from the Console goes out through `hid_write` unchanged, so start it with the report ID byte, `0x00` for devices without numbered reports. Serial Studio does not send feature reports.

### Frame parsing

Serial Studio does not read the report descriptor; you decode the bytes yourself. To see the layout, watch the Console in hex view while you move one control and note which byte changes, or read the descriptor in the firmware. Assume a gamepad whose report is 3 bytes: X (signed 8-bit), Y (signed 8-bit), then one byte of button bits. The parser is short:

```javascript
function parse(frame) {
    if (frame.length < 3) return [];
    var x = frame[0] > 127 ? frame[0] - 256 : frame[0];
    var y = frame[1] > 127 ? frame[1] - 256 : frame[1];
    return [x, y, frame[2]];
}
```

```bash
mosquitto_pub -h test.mosquitto.org -t yourname/sensor -m "23.5,48.2"
```

Two plots appear, one per comma-separated value. On an ESP32 or Arduino, any MQTT client library can publish the same line. With PubSubClient, after the usual Wi-Fi setup:

```cpp
WiFiClient net;
PubSubClient client(net);

client.setServer("test.mosquitto.org", 1883);
client.connect("yourname-esp32");
client.publish("yourname/sensor", "23.5,48.2");
```

## What an MQTT subscriber sees

The broker keeps a routing table. When a publisher posts to a topic, the broker forwards a copy of the payload to every client whose **topic filter** matches. The driver subscribes at QoS 0 and passes each matching payload to the [frame parser](Frame-Parser.md), the same one a UART source uses. Empty payloads and messages that do not match the filter are dropped.

Each poll now produces one binary frame, `[slave address, function code, byte count, data...]`, but the dashboard stays empty until datasets exist. In the Project Editor, select the source, open its **Frame Parser** tab, set the decoder to **Binary (Direct)**, paste a parser like this one and add four datasets, one per returned value. The frame is an array of byte values, and the parser joins each pair of data bytes into a 16-bit value:

```javascript
function parse(frame) {
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 3; i + 1 < frame.length; i += 2)
    out.push((frame[i] << 8) | frame[i + 1]);
  return out;
}
```

# Network Driver (TCP / UDP / WebSocket / HTTP)

Your ESP32 prints sensor values over Wi-Fi instead of USB. Point Serial Studio at its IP address and port, and each line it sends becomes a frame.

```cpp
#include <WiFi.h>

WiFiServer server(23);
WiFiClient client;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  WiFi.begin("my-ssid", "my-password");
  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) delay(250);
  Serial.println(WiFi.localIP());
  server.begin();
}

void loop() {
  if (!client || !client.connected()) client = server.available();
  if (client) client.println("1,2,3");
  delay(1000);
}
```

